        "text": "Lighthouses have a special place in the history of modern Australia. They stand as monuments to the country's change from a colonial outpost into a prosperous society. For millions of people, a lighthouse was the first sign of civilisation after a long sea voyage to a new home. For others, lighthouses are grim reminders of the dangers of the sea. Modern technology has made many lighthouses unnecessary, but the buildings still stir strong feelings. They remind us of a time when ships ruled the world.\n\nLights to help ships navigate have existed for more than 3,000 years. Their purpose has been to show ships where they are, to guide them into safe harbours, or to warn them about rocks and reefs that could destroy them. Saving lives has always been one consideration, but it is the preservation of ships and cargoes that has been the real driving force behind the building of lighthouses.\n\nLighthouses developed from a fire on a hilltop into towers built to resist anything the sea could throw at them, with beams of light that could be seen 50 km away. They reached their peak in the first half of the twentieth century, but by the end of that century their future had become uncertain. Today satellite navigation technology is taking the place of the lighthouse as the safe, economical and reliable way to navigate the world's oceans.\n\nThe first simple lights were fires in bronze baskets, used along the Nile delta as early as 1000 BC. The oldest lighthouse still standing is the Tower of Hercules, on a hill on the north-west coast of Spain. The Romans built it around 29 BC, and it worked as a lighthouse until the fifth century AD, when the Romans left the area and it was abandoned. The Spanish lit it again in 1682, and it has been in service ever since.\n\nItaly's best-known lighthouse is in Genoa. It was built in the 12th century, pulled down in 1544 and rebuilt as a brick tower in two sections. In 1449 one of its keepers was Antonio Columbus, the uncle of the more famous Christopher. The light at Meloria, also in Italy, was built in 1157 and was the first rock or wave-washed lighthouse. It no longer exists, but it was the forerunner of the many famous rock lighthouses of Brittany in France and of Great Britain.\n\nThe next challenge was to find a way to build towers in shallow water on a sandy seabed. This was solved by the development of pile lighthouses, made of either wood or iron, with the piles driven into the seabed. The first tower of this kind was built in 1841 at the mouth of the River Thames in London, but it was the USA that became the biggest user of this type of lighthouse.\n\nAccurate marine charts are now available for almost every part of the world's oceans. They have also been put onto computers, and these computerised charts, used together with GPS, can show the exact position of a ship on a screen. When they are connected to the ship's controls, they even allow it to sail on automatic pilot along any course planned in advance, in any kind of weather. The future is already here."
